Yes, we can provide the details for the SQL syntax check and unit test jobs.

For the sql-syntax-check job, this runs before the Liquibase deployment. It helps us validate the T-SQL queries that were newly created or changed within our repo by running them in parse-only mode against our existing database. This helps catch syntax issues earlier in the pipeline and provides a report with any syntax errors found.

For this job, we built a custom Docker image that uses Python as the base image and includes sqlcmd.

For the unit-tests job, we use two Docker images. This job spins up a SQL Server container, applies the DDL, executes all unit tests, and reports the pass/fail status.

The two Docker images used are:

* SQL Server image
* Custom Python-based image with pyodbc, pytest, pytest-html, and Flask

For the rollback job, we also have a custom rollback job that can take variables as input and run different Liquibase rollback commands based on the selected option.

For Zephyr upload, we use the XML report generated by the unit-tests job as input to upload the test execution results into Jira/Zephyr. We recently integrated the Zephyr upload job in the quality stage after the unit-tests job.

For SonarQube, we are currently using the Generic Sonar Component for T-SQL analysis. However, one limitation we found is that it does not provide line-level coverage for T-SQL source files. It would be helpful if the standard template could support or integrate with a tool that can provide line-level coverage for T-SQL queries.

